## Root Cause Investigation (A4.6)
The "issue" is the starter stub: `class Robot: def __init__(self): pass` provides no `name` attribute and no `reset()`. Phase 1 (root cause): stub is intentionally empty per the exercise harness — not a regression; there was no prior working behavior. Phase 3 (hypothesis): implementing the canonical class-level-uniqueness + `random`-generation pattern will satisfy all seven acceptance criteria. Phase 4 (implementation): RED test on the stub confirmed the failure mode (`AttributeError: 'Robot' object has no attribute 'name'`, 1/7 pass), then GREEN implementation passed 7/7 plus a 3000-robot/200-reset extended sweep.

**Implemented** (`robot_name.py`): `Robot.__init__` and `reset()` both call `_generate_name()`, which loops `random.choices(string.ascii_uppercase, k=2)` + `random.choices(string.digits, k=3)` until the candidate is absent from the class-level `_used_names` set, then registers it — guaranteeing format `AB123`, randomness, and cross-instance/cross-reset uniqueness.

**Verification:** spec-derived runner (kept in temp dir per the "no test files" constraint) → RED 1/7 on stub → GREEN 7/7 on implementation → `tests/verification_run.log`; `py_compile` clean; `script/linux/start.sh` + `restart.sh` smoke OK on final tree; `python3 tests/assert_artifacts.py --existing --backend-only` exit 0.

